WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.225
Local LLM load balancing means placing one stable endpoint in front of

00:00:04.225 --> 00:00:05.943
several independently running model-serving servers.

00:00:05.943 --> 00:00:10.755
Each backend still has its own runtime, model files, memory limits, network address, logs,

00:00:10.755 --> 00:00:11.786
and failure modes.

00:00:11.786 --> 00:00:14.880
The load balancer does not make those systems disappear.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:18.317
It decides which available backend should receive an incoming request,

00:00:18.317 --> 00:00:21.410
while clients continue to call one predictable service address.

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:24.160
That is different from distributed inference, model parallelism,

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:28.628
or a true cluster designed to split one inference job across multiple machines.

00:00:28.628 --> 00:00:32.066
A normal load balancer sends one request to one backend.

00:00:32.066 --> 00:00:35.159
Specialized distributed inference can divide work across compute resources,

00:00:35.159 --> 00:00:37.221
but it requires specific runtime support.

00:00:37.221 --> 00:00:41.346
In this course, the focus is practical routing across local inference endpoints,

00:00:41.346 --> 00:00:43.752
not combining machines into one larger model.

00:00:43.752 --> 00:00:46.502
The most important expectation is latency versus throughput.

00:00:46.502 --> 00:00:48.908
Latency is the delay for one request,

00:00:48.908 --> 00:00:52.345
such as time to first token and total response time.

00:00:52.345 --> 00:00:55.782
Throughput is how much work the service completes over time.

00:00:55.782 --> 00:00:59.563
Two servers can increase total throughput when several requests are active,

00:00:59.563 --> 00:01:03.687
but they usually do not make a single response twice as fast

00:01:03.687 --> 00:01:06.437
because one backend is still generating that response.

00:01:06.437 --> 00:01:10.562
Load balancing is useful for labs, coding assistants, chat UIs, batch jobs,

00:01:10.562 --> 00:01:16.061
and small team environments because it gives tools one endpoint while the backend pool can change.

00:01:16.061 --> 00:01:18.811
Used well, it improves availability and spreads demand.

00:01:18.811 --> 00:01:22.592
Used poorly, it can hide overload, route requests to incompatible models,

00:01:22.592 --> 00:01:23.623
break streaming behavior,

00:01:23.623 --> 00:01:27.060
or expose a sensitive local inference service without proper controls.
